WEBVTT

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:13.560
As Apollo 11 embarked on mankind's bold adventure to land on the moon,

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:21.680
worlds, hopes, and dreams hung on the actions of its three-man crew,

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.980
especially Commander Neil Armstrong,

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:29.980
with the skills that had made him one of America's...

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.330
of his finest aviators, Armstrong marked his place in history with these famous words.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.140
Headstand for man, march to sleep.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.460
But what was it that had brought him to this historic moment?

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:56.720
Armstrong had climbed to the pinnacle of his profession, tested in combat in the skies over Korea.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:17.640
Reaching to the limit of the atmosphere as an elite test pilot and on into space where his cool head saved lives and finally accepting a lifelong mantle of fame that didn't always sit well with him.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:25.720
We ask a lot of our heroes. We put a burden on them, put a burden on Neil Armstrong that he didn't enjoy.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.040
So who was Neil Armstrong?

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:35.580
His story now told by those who lived, loved, and worked with

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:40.110
the first man on the moon, up next on NOVA.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:20.280
that this nation should commit itself to achieving the goal before this decade is out of landing

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:25.800
a man on the moon and returning him safely to the earth. When President John F. Kennedy

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.620
articulated this bold vision in 1961.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:36.500
He pinned American technological supremacy and national pride

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.690
on winning a race to the moon.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:41.660
The stakes were huge.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.660
It staggers the imagination, frankly,

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.860
and there were plenty of people, even within NASA,

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.640
who thought that Kennedy had lost his sanity.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:02:59.750
Incredibly.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:05.280
Just eight years later, three men were poised to achieve the president's goal.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.990
In command was Neil Armstrong.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:17.100
We, the crew of Apollo 11, are privileged to represent the United States

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:24.210
in our first attempt to take man to another heavenly body.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.750
At 38 years old...

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.300
Armstrong was at the pinnacle of an impressive flying career.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:39.670
An innate steadiness, along with exceptional aviation skills,

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:42.130
had seen him through the Korean War,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.990
allowed him to master the most unforgiving aircraft as a test pilot,

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.260
and brought a crippled spacecraft safely back to Earth.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.430
Now, his ability as a pilot would be put to the ultimate test.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:07.150
attempting a landing on the moon as the world held its breath.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:09.590
And with only seconds of fuel remaining,

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:15.380
Neil Armstrong guided his fragile craft towards the surface of an alien world.

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:23.730
He was about to complete a journey that for him had begun more than 30 years before

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:27.350
when he had first taken flight as a young boy.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:38.240
Born here in Wapakoneta, Ohio, on August 5, 1930,

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:42.460
Neil Armstrong's love affair with flying began early,

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.110
as his childhood friend recalls.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.380
When he was like five years old,

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:54.070
his father took him on an airplane ride on a tri-motor.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:58.910
Dad got sick, but Neil just absolutely loved it.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:04.610
The mid-1930s was a golden age of flight in America,

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:09.240
and like many other young children,

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:14.160
Neil's first taste of being airborne left a lasting impression.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:21.830
This was the start and the feeling of being airborne

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:26.410
and actually flying like a bird.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:29.220
It kindled.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.020
his inspiration to fly.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:39.720
He absolutely loved everything about flight.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.520
He would have three or four model airplane projects

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:48.830
going on all the time, mostly gliders.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.220
Then he got into the rubber band type

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:56.880
and he just kept building bigger and bigger ones

00:05:57.970 --> 00:05:59.910
and better ones.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:12.660
We both made models early, and of course our desire then, as it was later in our careers, was to make these things go higher and faster.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:19.640
And my solution to higher and faster was you took a couple of extra turns on the rubber band.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:24.710
Neil's solution, he built a wind tunnel.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:29.970
When we were ready for the test, he...

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:37.360
said, go get mom. I said, Neil wants you to see something, so, and he turned it on. And

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:48.140
all of a sudden the house shook, and I mean the house really shook. How many kids could

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:59.940
build a wind tunnel in their basement? Not any that I know except Neil. Neil's in

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:06.170
The infatuation with flying was fueled as America entered the Second World War in December 1941.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:16.200
He devoured the daring exploits of Allied pilots portrayed in popular wartime magazines.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:23.490
They inspired him, and at just 15 years old, he learned to fly.

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:28.980
He had his pilot's license before he had his driver's license.

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:34.980
During the war, developments in aviation were moving fast.

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:43.230
After 1945, propeller planes were starting to be replaced by aircraft powered by jet and rocket engines.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:52.920
Then came an event that shook the aviation world.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:07:59.710
Chuck Yeager breaking the speed of sound.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.690
his Bell X-1 rocket plane in 1947 coincided with an ominous turn in east-west relations,

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:12.390
and the implications for Armstrong would prove profound.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:20.150
Eager to pursue a career in aeronautical engineering, Armstrong won a Navy scholarship to study

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:22.850
the subject and enrolled at Purdue University.

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:28.680
But his studies were soon interrupted as the Cold War began to heat up.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:35.030
At the end of his second year, which would have been 1950, the Korean War started.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:41.560
Backed by communist China, North Korea invaded South Korea.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:49.110
When America responded by scrambling its armed forces, Armstrong found himself at war.

00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:51.330
He was 20 years old.

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:59.970
Join Naval Fighter Squadron VF-51 on the aircraft carrier U.S.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:08.680
USS Essex. There was a lot to learn, and fast, as he recalls in this audio interview.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:16.900
We had to become carrier qualified in the jet aircraft, doing a lot of practice with weapons

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:26.450
delivery, instrument flying, and so on. I was very young, very green. But Armstrong quickly

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:29.900
mastered carrier flying, one of aviation's most challenging

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.770
jobs and was soon showing his skill in combat.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:40.960
One of his jobs was to dive bomb and blow up bridges and

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:48.780
railroads, and he said that North Koreans strung up wires.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:54.320
Low-flying pilots, anti-aircraft cables were an

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:58.540
ever-present danger. They were hard to spot, even for the

00:09:58.540 --> 00:09:59.480
sharp-eyed Armstrong.

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:05.670
I actually ran through a cable, and an anti-aircraft cable.

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:09.710
Knocked off about six or eight feet of my right wing.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:16.080
Battling to keep control, Armstrong needed to think fast and react quickly.

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:21.950
As long as he could keep a certain speed, he could stay up.

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:23.810
But as soon as it slowed down, the plane would drop.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:26.590
And so he knew that he could not land on the aircraft carrier.

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:27.630
He'd have to bail out.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:29.980
This close...

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:35.680
revealed Armstrong's uncanny ability to always remain calm under pressure.

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:44.540
He never showed any fear or anything involving his close calls.

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:51.400
He really loved what he was doing. It was a very meaningful time for him.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:59.600
The Korean War sharpened the skills of many young pilots, including Armstrong.

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:05.230
He'd flown 78 missions by the age of 22.

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:10.720
He returned to Purdue in 1952,

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:16.380
where he received his degree and found a wife.

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:18.560
Well, I met him at Purdue.

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:23.480
He told someone that I was the one he was going to marry,

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:28.310
but he never asked me out until he had graduated.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:36.780
We were married in January in 1956, and after that, in May, we went up to the desert.

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:45.440
Here at Edwards Air Force Base in California, Armstrong would become a test pilot.

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:57.690
Edwards was the mecca for America's elite aviators, but the work wasn't for the faint-hearted.

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:12.470
It required a cool head, quick thinking, and the ability to understand how an untested machine would react in an untried environment.

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:22.180
Honing these skills would make test pilots top contenders for future space missions, and Armstrong was no exception.

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:27.700
We were out at the edges of the flight envelope all the time, testing limits.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:46.020
Memory serves. There were 17 aircraft, pretty much all different, of ex-airplanes and fighters and a B-47 and a couple of B-29s and all kinds of exotic aircraft.

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:57.370
And as they became more confident of my abilities, they gave me more and more jobs, and I did a lot of different test programs in those days.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.930
The kinds of flying that he did at Edwards really put him in the elite top of the test

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:07.050
flying fraternity.

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:13.380
But one machine at Edwards pushed Armstrong higher and faster than any other.

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:17.230
The X-15.

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:25.440
The X-15 was literally crossing the boundary from aviation into spaceflight.

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:29.900
And it was an incredibly demanding vehicle to fly.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:37.920
Half plane, half spacecraft, the rocket-powered X-15 was the cutting edge of aviation technology.

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:46.830
It flew at hypersonic speeds, more than six times faster than sound, soaring over 50 miles in altitude.

00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:51.670
It still holds the record of the fastest plane ever flown.

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:55.210
The X-15 was absolutely the top of the line.

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:59.830
It was a whole supersonic zone above.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.580
the rest of us and therefore all the people who flew the X-15 were held in

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:10.640
the highest regard by the rest of us peasants and Neil of course was one of

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:17.720
that group. That was a very exciting program. Challenging goals. I think it

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:24.750
was certainly one of the memorable parts of my life. One flight almost got the

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:25.310
better of it.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:42.080
I got the nose up above the horizon, and I found I was actually, you know, skipping outside of the atmosphere.

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:44.120
I had no aerodynamic controls.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:52.770
Soaring out of the atmosphere at almost a mile a second, Armstrong was unable to keep control.

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:58.690
What I couldn't do is get back down in the atmosphere.

00:14:58.990 --> 00:14:59.980
I pulled up.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.080
over and pulled down, but it wasn't going down because there was no air to bite into.

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:14.480
So I just had to wait until I got back in with enough air to have aerodynamic control and

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:19.580
some lift on the wings, and immediately started making a turn back.

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:27.020
He's the essence of the engineering test pilot, and what that carries with it is an

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:29.860
intensity of focus like you can't imagine.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:44.660
it the x-15 further challenged and sharpened armstrong's flying ability but his young family

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:51.840
also faced challenges at edwards it was totally different foreign to anything i've ever known in

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:59.220
my life that's where we lived when rick was born and then

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.830
Shortly thereafter, Karen.

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:07.300
In 1961, age two, Karen fell seriously ill.

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:20.660
Karen was a precious thing, and she developed a tumor in her brain, and we could not save her.

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:25.040
The death of Karen really hurt him.

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:29.200
It was the only time that I had ever seen him.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.990
Really, really hurt. Couldn't talk about it.

00:16:35.530 --> 00:16:38.100
Despite his loss, to all outward appearances,

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:41.880
Armstrong remained focused on his duties as a test pilot.

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:46.490
But beyond the skies at Edwards, the space race was on,

00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:57.260
opening up an entirely new set of opportunities.

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:07.790
America's manned space program began with Project Mercury in 1961.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:12.540
Six short flights, each carrying a single astronaut.

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:23.880
But to meet President Kennedy's challenge of landing a man on the moon by the end of the decade,

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:27.250
NASA would require more astronauts.

00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:29.980
When they

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.020
NASA was looking, you know, Neil Armstrong was at the top of their list

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:37.440
because he'd had all of that flight test experience at Edwards,

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:42.420
and that just made him incredibly attractive to the astronaut selection group.

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:48.730
Curiously, the Milwaukee Journal gave me a call,

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:56.200
and they said, I understand your brother is one of the newest astronauts.

00:17:58.410 --> 00:17:59.980
I was, I think I was...

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:11.060
speechless along with Janet Rick and a new son Mark Neil began a new life in

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:18.000
Houston the home of America's manned space program it was a nice house it had

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:21.690
a you know we had a pool you know because it was Houston because it was

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:29.660
often very hot there was a lot of swimming the neighborhood was buzzing with

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:29.880
train

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:36.800
astronauts. There was this guy in the backyard in front of the garage where

00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:45.200
there's a lot of cement and here's this guy roller skating. I said who's that?

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:59.960
They said oh that's Neil Armstrong. By 1964 NASA's blueprint to reach the

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.300
moon was taking shape, as this animated film of the time shows. It was called

00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:15.270
Project Apollo. The plan went like this. Guzzling 15 tons of fuel a second at

00:19:15.270 --> 00:19:20.340
launch, the giant Saturn V rocket would send the Apollo spacecraft, both the

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:26.960
command and lunar module, into space. After about 69 hours, they go into orbit

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.980
around the moon. Once there, the spacecraft

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:36.460
spacecraft undock, the command module remains in orbit while the lunar module attempts the landing.

00:19:38.630 --> 00:19:43.850
After exploring the surface, the two astronauts rejoin their companion in lunar orbit.

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:51.120
Finally, they leave lunar orbit and make the trip back to Earth, and the mission ends with

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:54.800
the command module re-entering the Earth's atmosphere and splashing down in the Pacific.

00:19:56.290 --> 00:19:59.260
It looked great on paper, but could it work?

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:04.620
Finding out was the task of Project Gemini.

00:20:08.610 --> 00:20:10.890
The demands of a lunar mission were so great.

00:20:11.050 --> 00:20:13.070
You had to learn how to rendezvous in space.

00:20:14.030 --> 00:20:16.250
You had to keep people happy and healthy

00:20:16.250 --> 00:20:18.030
for up to two weeks in space.

00:20:19.760 --> 00:20:22.500
They had to be able to work in the vacuum of space,

00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:24.740
in a space suit, a pressurized suit.

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:27.620
It's like a million dollars.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:35.440
So Gemini was really the way that NASA could learn to master these complexities

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:37.940
in the relative safety of low-Earth orbit.

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:43.680
Armstrong's first spaceflight was Gemini 8 in 1966,

00:20:44.750 --> 00:20:48.110
a daring mission to attempt the first docking in space

00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:51.310
with an unmanned spacecraft called Agena.

00:20:52.290 --> 00:20:54.250
His co-pilot was Dave Scott.

00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:57.730
Well, yes, I mean, the whole program depended on docking.

00:20:58.310 --> 00:20:59.470
Docking had to be proven.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.240
or we couldn't go to the moon.

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:03.520
So it was a critical mission, yeah.

00:21:05.720 --> 00:21:08.160
Squeezed into their tight-fitting Gemini capsule,

00:21:08.770 --> 00:21:10.410
the pair prepared for launch.

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:13.590
Neither of them knew what lay in store.

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:39.680
Well, in our homes during flight, we had air-to-ground communications.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:42.840
We called them the squawk box because it squawked all the time.

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:49.770
When they talked air-to-ground, you could update yourself.

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:52.800
They started out just great.

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:07.980
Their docking target, the Agena rocket, had been launched earlier that day.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:21.520
As Armstrong and Scott passed into the night side of the earth, they prepared for docking.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:41.530
But within half an hour, Scott realized there was something wrong.

00:22:41.980 --> 00:22:44.300
You're supposed to fly straight and level like an airplane,

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:46.940
but all of a sudden I noticed that we were tilted.

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:51.500
They didn't know it, but a small maneuvering thruster

00:22:51.500 --> 00:22:55.630
on their Gemini spacecraft had become stuck and was firing.

00:22:57.480 --> 00:22:59.980
We first suspected that the Agena was the...

00:23:00.810 --> 00:23:02.430
We were on the dark side of the Earth,

00:23:02.570 --> 00:23:05.330
so we really didn't have any outside reference.

00:23:06.190 --> 00:23:07.870
Out of contact with the ground,

00:23:08.110 --> 00:23:10.710
the astronauts struggled to regain control.

00:23:12.820 --> 00:23:14.420
So I said, Neil, we better get off.

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:15.660
He said, yeah, we better get off.

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:16.940
Let's prepare to undock.

00:23:17.060 --> 00:23:22.940
And then he says, ready, and I put my hand on the switch,

00:23:23.060 --> 00:23:23.940
and Neil says, undock.

00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:28.530
And then things start really moving.

00:23:39.050 --> 00:23:42.510
Then we go into a very rapid roll, which was almost a tumble.

00:23:43.020 --> 00:23:48.000
And at that point we realized that it wasn't the Agena, it must be the Gemini.

00:23:48.280 --> 00:23:52.900
They were spinning at maybe a revolution per second.

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:59.680
At home, a photographer from Life magazine captured Janet as she listened to the unfolding

00:23:59.680 --> 00:23:59.980
drama.

00:24:01.460 --> 00:24:06.200
And there was a very strong concern that they would black out.

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:08.810
And that would be it. It would be over.

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:15.390
Then NASA cut the squawk box.

00:24:15.590 --> 00:24:18.360
I didn't like that.

00:24:19.620 --> 00:24:26.080
So I went over to NASA and I was refused entry.

00:24:31.630 --> 00:24:36.550
Back in orbit, Armstrong kept his cool, figuring out his only remaining option,

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:41.760
disengage all the maneuvering thrusters, including the one that was stuck,

00:24:42.140 --> 00:24:48.020
and use the re-entry thrusters to counteract the tumbling and regain control of the spacecraft.

00:24:48.840 --> 00:24:54.460
He had to reach up above his head and throw switches under this high-speed roll.

00:24:55.040 --> 00:24:59.800
That's amazing that he was able to do that, and he knew exactly where the switches were, exactly.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:13.540
which ones to throw. I mean, the guy was brilliant. He knew the system so well that he found a

00:25:13.540 --> 00:25:20.080
solution. He activated the solution under extreme circumstances. And I got to say, it was my lucky

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:22.260
day to be flying with Mr. Neil Armstrong.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:39.820
the re-entry thrusters meant aborting the mission and a couple of hours later the crew splashed down

00:25:39.820 --> 00:25:49.950
in the south china sea armstrong had cut short the flight but he'd saved their lives he landed

00:25:49.950 --> 00:25:56.980
and came home you know he's telling me about the flight we knew that they could have lost their life

00:25:58.100 --> 00:25:59.660
and you knew that anyway so

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:02.420
